{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2085404187/5/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"da515329\" lines=\"11-24\">\n<description>\nGrow four clockwise spiral wall paths from the tips of a teal cross.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = 0 (black). A single teal (8) cross centered in the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each arm tip launches a \"turtle\" heading 90° right of its outward direction.\n- Movement priority: turn right > go straight > turn left (left only if not on border).\n- Normal step = 2 cells; step right after a turn = 1 cell.\n- Each new cell must have a 1-cell orthogonal gap from all existing walls except the predecessor.\n\nKey insight: The right-turn preference plus alternating 2/1 step lengths creates self-avoiding spirals that tile the grid with uniform spacing.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b5ca7ac4\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nMove bordered 5×5 squares to their designated grid edges by border color.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Azure (8) and red (2) bordered 5×5 squares with colored interiors, scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Azure squares → left edge (col 0); red squares → right edge (col W−5).\n- Each square keeps its original row.\n- Process azure left-to-right, red right-to-left (nearest-to-edge first).\n- If a square's rows overlap an already-placed edge square, place it one square-width inward instead.\n\nKey insight: Near-edge-first ordering plus row-overlap determines which square sits at the edge vs. the second column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"db695cfb\" lines=\"8-19\">\n<description>\nConnect same-color diagonal pixel pairs, then emit perpendicular rays from differently-colored pixels on those diagonals.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Line color (e.g. blue) has pixel pairs aligned on shared diagonals. Trigger color (e.g. magenta) pixels sit along those diagonals.\n\nRule:\n- Draw diagonal lines connecting each pair of same-color aligned pixels.\n- Where the diagonal crosses a trigger pixel, emit perpendicular diagonal rays (in the trigger's color) extending to the grid boundary.\n\nKey insight: Trigger pixels act as beam-splitters — the perpendicular rays rotate 90° from the connecting diagonal's orientation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"16de56c4\" lines=\"9-34\">\n<description>\nFill periodic progressions that shoot from a unique grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.  Sparse non-bg dots of several colors.\n- All repeating colors originate from the same grid edge (top, bottom,\n  left, or right — the one with the most non-bg dots).\n- Each repeating color has exactly 2 dots per shooting-direction line;\n  their gap defines the step.\n- Every non-bg dot lies on a shooting-direction line that contains a\n  repeating color.\n\nRule:\n- Determine the unique shooting edge; left/right → rows, top/bottom → columns.\n- For each shooting-direction line, the repeating color has exactly 2 dots;\n  step = gap between them, offset = first position mod step.\n- A different-colored dot aligned to the same beat (pos % step == offset) is\n  a companion: it absorbs the ray and becomes the fill color, limiting the\n  fill range to the span between repeating and companion dots.\n- An unaligned dot is a bystander and left untouched.\n- Without a companion the repeating color fills the entire line.\n\nKey insight: the shooting edge is identified by counting boundary dots;\nthe gap between the two marker dots is the period; a companion sharing the\nmodular class acts as a wall that stops the ray and dictates the paint color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"62593bfd\" lines=\"32-46\">\n<description>\nSort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n\nKey insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"fc7cae8d\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nAlign a central rectangle to two colored frame-edge controls via rotation/reflection.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with two solid-colored border edges (distinct non-bg colors).\n- A central filled rectangle with 1px colored guide edges on two sides, colors matching the frame controls.\n\nRule:\n- Rotate/mirror the object so its guide edges face the same sides as the matching frame controls.\n- Crop off the 1px guide edges from aligned sides.\n\nKey insight: Guide-edge colors encode target frame sides; the correct dihedral transform matches both simultaneously.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"45a5af55\" lines=\"5-18\">\n<description>\nBuild nested square frames from a stack of horizontal color stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- Full-width horizontal stripes of distinct non-bg colors, stacked vertically.\n- Each stripe has uniform color and variable height (1-4 rows).\n\nRule:\n- Read stripes bottom to top.\n- Bottom stripe becomes the center solid square with side length = stripe height.\n- Each subsequent stripe wraps the current shape with a square frame of thickness = stripe height, in that stripe's color.\n\nKey insight: Stripe height doubles as both frame thickness and (for the innermost) center-square side length; output dimensions are fully determined by the stripe heights.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3a25b0d8\" lines=\"9-22\">\n<description>\nFill holes in a target object with colors from a source object's subsprites, then tight-crop.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Two similar 8-connected objects share an outline color.\n- Source has colored subsprites in cavities; target has empty cavities.\n\nRule:\n- Find the two 8-connected objects.\n- Target = object with more hole area. Align subsprites to holes (rotation/flip if needed).\n- Fill each hole with its matched subsprite color; tight-crop.\n\nKey insight: Hole centers in the target are matched to source subsprite centers under the best rotation/flip transform.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6ffbe589\" lines=\"83-98\">\n<description>\nCrop a board cluster and rotate its color layers based on outside cue sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A main multi-color board cluster (largest foreground region).\n- Small monochrome cue sprites outside the board for the colors that rotate.\n\nRule:\n- Tight-crop around the board cluster.\n- For each color layer inside the crop, find its same-color cue outside.\n- Rotate that layer clockwise by (cue pixel count mod 4) × 90°.\n- No outside cue → no rotation.\n\nKey insight: Cue sprite size mod 4 encodes rotation amount for its matching color layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"c4d067a0\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nComplete a grid of colored blocks to match a minimap legend in the top-left corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Top-left corner: a small sprite of single colored pixels forming a minimap pattern on a regular lattice.\n- Main area: uniformly colored square blocks (NxN) arranged on a regular grid; some positions are empty (bg).\n\nRule:\n- The legend pixels define a color pattern; each pixel's lattice position maps to a block position in the main grid via a fixed scale factor and offset.\n- Existing colored blocks confirm the scale/offset alignment.\n- For every legend pixel whose corresponding grid position is empty, place a new block of that color.\n\nKey insight: The top-left pixel pattern is a scaled-down minimap of the block layout; the mapping is inferred from blocks already present.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"f560132c\" lines=\"259-273\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the \"pieces\"), scattered across the grid.\n- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.\n\nRule:\n- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.\n- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.\n- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.\n\nKey insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"78332cb0\" lines=\"10-23\">\n<description>\nReorder 5×5 blocks into a connected strip by matching edge foreground pixels.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background; sep = divider color (full row/column lines).\n- Several 5×5 sprite blocks in a grid, separated by sep lines.\n\nRule:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
